Compute btrfs snapshot usage from quota accounting instead of walking

The btrfs snapshotter sizes an active snapshot by walking it recursively.
Because every layer is created as `btrfs subvolume snapshot <parent>`, layer N
physically contains layers 1..N, so a stats collection pass costs
O(layers x rootfs) stat() calls rather than O(unique files).  On a six-node
arm64 cluster with ~40 containers per node that is 1.5M stat() calls per pass,
8-9 minutes of wall clock, and a permanent ~47% of one core per node.

btrfs already accounts for exactly the quantity the snapshotter wants.  The
level 0 qgroup btrfs creates alongside each subvolume tracks the extents only
that subvolume references, which for a snapshot is what has been written since
it was taken -- the same number fs.DiffUsage walks the tree to compute.  Reading
it is a single tree search ioctl pinned to one key.

Simple quotas (`btrfs quota enable --simple`, Linux 6.7+) make this cheap on
both ends: extents are charged to the subvolume that created them at write
time, with no backref walk and no rescan, unlike full qgroups.

Adds a `usage_mode` option to the btrfs snapshotter plugin:

  auto   - quota accounting when the filesystem has quotas enabled, walk
           otherwise (default; preserves current behaviour on filesystems
           without quotas)
  qgroup - always quota accounting, refuse to start without quotas
  walk   - always walk, the pre-patch behaviour
  none   - report zero usage without measuring anything

Usage() never forces a transaction commit, so stats collection stays cheap;
Commit() syncs first, since the value it computes is persisted.  Inodes are
reported as zero because btrfs accounts bytes, not inodes.

Two things to know when enabling quotas on a populated filesystem: simple
quotas only account for extents written after they were turned on, so snapshots
that already existed report zero until they are re-created, and a subvolume
that has no qgroup at all is walked rather than reported as zero.

Reported-by and tested-on: OrangePi 5 Plus (rk3588) Gentoo k8s cluster.

Upstream-Issue: https://github.com/containerd/containerd/issues/13967
Based-on: https://github.com/containerd/containerd/pull/1836 (AkihiroSuda,
  the qgroup approach; reworked for containerd 2.x, single-key lookups,
  squota detection, no implicit quota enable, and a configurable mode)
Related: https://github.com/containerd/containerd/issues/6067
Related: https://github.com/containerd/containerd/issues/4217

